    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d551">

                <pb facs="0615" n="517" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg517"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1111 Nelson, Bert N./147</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister in China (Johnson) to the Chinese Minister
                        for Foreign Affairs (Lo)</hi><anchor n="59" xml:id="d551-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 439</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-06-01">June 1, 1932</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: I have the honor to refer to Your
                    Excellency’s formal note of April 22, 1932,<anchor n="60" xml:id="d551-f2"/> in
                    which you were good enough to inform me that the Honan Provincial Government had
                    issued instructions that prompt action be taken in connection with the robbery
                    of money and supplies near Kwangshan.</p>
                <pb facs="0616" n="518" xml:id="pg518"/>
                <p>I am now in receipt of a further report from the American Consul General at
                        Hankow<anchor n="62" xml:id="d551-f3"/> in which he states that
                        Ch’iu Chu-hsien and the others involved in the robbery
                    are still at large and that the present Magistrate has taken no action whatever.
                    It appears to the Legation that the issuance of general instructions is
                    productive of absolutely no result and it is, therefore, requested that specific
                    instructions be issued ordering the chief of the Kwangshan militia to proceed to
                    Kaifeng for investigation. I cannot but again urge upon Your Excellency the
                    imperative necessity for effective action in this matter.</p>
                <p>I avail myself [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nelson Trusler Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
